# ClipIQ

Local-first, reviewable video highlights. ClipIQ proposes events using audio,
visual activity and optional speech, refines their boundaries, then exports
playable clips. Every proposal needs human review: scores are relative heuristics,
not virality probabilities or semantic guarantees.

## Install on macOS / Apple Silicon

Use Python 3.12 and FFmpeg. Existing project environments can be reused.

```bash
cd /Users/venkatasushalnalanagu/clipiq
# Only if FFmpeg is absent:
brew install ffmpeg
# Only if the environment does not already exist:
python3.12 -m venv .venv312
./.venv312/bin/python -m pip install -e .
```

`requirements.txt` points to the same dependencies as `pyproject.toml`. It no
longer maintains an independent exact-version list. Python 3.10+ is required;
3.12 is the tested version. The UI uses Python's standard library.

For local transcription, if not already installed:

```bash
./.venv312/bin/python -m pip install -e '.[speech]'
```

Installing speech libraries does not download a model. The new `run` command
requires an existing Whisper model or timestamped transcript, unless you
explicitly pass `--allow-model-download`. `--no-speech` runs without Whisper.
No cloud API, key or subscription is needed. Legacy APIs retain their original
model-loading behavior.

## Start the review application

```bash
cd /Users/venkatasushalnalanagu/clipiq
PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv312/bin/python -m clipiq review --output data/processed/clipiq
```

Open **http://127.0.0.1:8765**. Choose a video from `data/raw` suggestions or enter
its full local path. Optionally enter the existing `data/processed` folder to
adopt compatible legacy caches. Start processing, compare original and proposed
intervals, adjust start/end, preview, approve/export, reject, or download.
Approval creates a fresh clip and immutable decision file; it never alters the
original annotations. The server binds only to loopback. Stop with Ctrl+C.

The UI accepts paths on the same machine as the server; it does not upload videos
to any service. Jobs run in the server process; keep the terminal open. If it is
stopped, restart and rerun: completed analysis stages are reusable.

## Process a video without a notebook

For a video at `data/raw/test_video2.mp4` with existing corresponding cache files:

```bash
PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv312/bin/python -m clipiq run data/raw/test_video2.mp4 \
  --output data/processed/clipiq --reuse-legacy data/processed \
  --top-k 5 --max-duration 60
```

Replace the video path if your source is elsewhere. `--reuse-legacy` explicitly
asserts that `<video-stem>_audio_features.json`, `_visual_features.json`, and
`_transcription.json` in that directory belong to this video. Legacy files have
no verified source fingerprint. Their schemas, timestamps and durations are
validated; incompatible feature files are recomputed. Aggregate
`combined_features.json` is never treated as a candidate table. Detailed ranked
candidates are regenerated and persisted automatically. An explicitly supplied
invalid transcript fails clearly rather than silently substituting another video.

Other modes:

```bash
# No transcription or model download:
PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv312/bin/python -m clipiq run /path/to/video.mp4 --no-speech
# Use a known timestamped transcript:
PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv312/bin/python -m clipiq run /path/to/video.mp4 --transcript /path/to/transcription.json
# Explicitly permit the selected Whisper model download:
PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv312/bin/python -m clipiq run /path/to/video.mp4 --model base --allow-model-download
# Produce additional full-frame 9:16 exports and segment-timed subtitles:
PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv312/bin/python -m clipiq run /path/to/video.mp4 --portrait --captions
```

`--force` recomputes the analysis stages. `--analyze-only` writes proposals and
reports without rendering. Portrait exports use padding to keep the entire
original frame visible: subject-aware cropping is not implemented. Captions are
SRT sidecars using segment timing, not word-aligned or burned-in captions. When
speech is absent, clips still export and the caption limitation is recorded.

## Where highlights are saved

Every run uses a unique folder, including when the same video is processed again:

```text
data/processed/clipiq/
  cache/                           # content-addressed stage results and WAV
  latest.json                      # pointer to the latest finished run
  runs/<UTC-timestamp>_<unique-id>/
    manifest.json                  # parameters, cache hits, timings, errors, clips
    transcription.json
    baseline/                      # both original strategy tables for comparison
    reports/boundary_proposals.json
    reports/boundary_proposals.csv
    clips/highlight_001.mp4         # original aspect ratio
    clips/highlight_001.srt         # optional segment captions
    portrait/highlight_001.mp4      # optional padded 9:16
    reviews/<decision-id>.json      # immutable approve/reject/preview decisions
    reviews/<decision-id>.mp4       # approved or adjusted preview exports
```

The command prints the exact run directory. A failed job retains a manifest with
its error. Partial export failure returns a nonzero exit status and leaves any
successful clips intact. FFmpeg render durations are checked with ffprobe; normal
frame/audio quantization up to 0.3 seconds is allowed. Exported proposals stay
within the requested duration budget before frame quantization. Short fragments
under two seconds remain review proposals but are excluded from automatic export.

## Compatibility and architecture

The existing `process_video(...)`, `run_clipiq(...)`, and
`python -m clipiq VIDEO --output ...` interfaces remain available for baseline
workflows. The new **`run` subcommand is the recommended integrated workflow**.
The original positional CLI does not gain caching or refined selection; migrate
by inserting `run` before the video argument. Existing notebook variables are
never needed by the new commands.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  V[Source video] --> C[Content fingerprint + stage cache]
  C --> A[Audio RMS / visual activity / optional transcript]
  A --> P[Shared existing candidate and heuristic ranking pipeline]
  P --> B[Evidence continuity + existing boundary refiner]
  B --> S[Bounded segmentation + deduplication + diversity]
  S --> E[Validated FFmpeg exports + JSON/CSV manifests]
  E --> R[Local review UI]
  R --> D[Immutable decisions + adjusted exports]
```

Feature timelines and DataFrames round-trip through tagged JSON, never pickle.
Cache files use checksums and atomic replacement. Source bytes, stage version,
parameters and relevant model/dependency information determine cache keys.
Visual decoding streams frames. RMS extraction bounds waveform memory; timelines
still grow with duration. Whisper itself may load the entire audio file, so very
long speech-enabled videos can still require substantial RAM.

Continuity requires shared observed evidence; proximity alone is insufficient.
Long events prefer transcript ends or low-motion cuts outside speech, with forced
cuts explicitly flagged when necessary. Deduplication requires both temporal
and event similarity. Brief nested payoffs and distinct overlapping events are
preserved. Completeness, context and diversity adjustments are heuristic.
Wordless excitement and motorsport candidates can use continuing activity to
extend an ending, but the system cannot know that a race finish or story payoff
is complete. All proposals retain uncertainty and review flags.

`selection.SemanticAdapter` is an optional Python interface for a caller-supplied
local model. It is not loaded automatically and failures fall back to heuristics.
No concrete LLM/VLM backend or large model is bundled. The trained classifier
mentioned in the project brief was not in the supplied source; it has not been
replaced, retrained or evaluated by this update.

## Tests and local video validation

```bash
PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv312/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
# Locate Airsoft, Motorsport and Minecraft under data/, reuse matching caches,
# run the suite and each video in a separate Python process:
./.venv312/bin/python tools/validate_local.py
# Or just Airsoft:
./.venv312/bin/python tools/validate_local.py --video-name test_video2.mp4
```

The local validator writes logs and `real_video_results.json` in a new
`work/validation_<timestamp>/` directory. Missing/ambiguous videos or failed runs
produce a nonzero exit status. It never downloads a Whisper model automatically.
Use `--no-speech` only when intentionally testing action/audio without speech.

See `IMPLEMENTATION_REPORT.md` for measured synthetic results, testing scope and
remaining limits. No real-video quality improvement is claimed without the
source media, annotations and trained baseline artifact.
